<p></p>
<h1 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-Overview">Overview</h1>
<p>This page outlines the best practices for writing clean, maintainable, a=
nd efficient code. Following these guidelines will help ensure consistency,=
 readability, and quality across all projects.</p>
<h1 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-GeneralPrinciples">General Principles</h1>
<ol>
<li><strong>Write Readable Code</strong>: Code should be self-explanatory a=
nd easy to understand.</li>
<li><strong>DRY Principle</strong>: Avoid duplicating code; reuse wherever =
possible.</li>
<li><strong>Keep It Simple (KISS)</strong>: Avoid overcomplicating solution=
s; aim for simplicity.</li>
<li><strong>YAGNI (You Aren=E2=80=99t Gonna Need It)</strong>: Only impleme=
nt what is required, avoid speculative features.</li>
<li><strong>SOLID Principles</strong>: <span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,0);">F=
ollow</span> <a href=3D"https://www.digitalocean.com/community/conceptual-a=
rticles/s-o-l-i-d-the-first-five-principles-of-object-oriented-design" clas=
s=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">SOLID</a> principles</li>
<li><span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,0);"><strong>Boys Scout Rule</strong>: <s=
pan style=3D"text-decoration: none;">Always leave the campground cleaner th=
an you found it.</span></span></li>
</ol>
<h1 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-References"><span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,=
0);"><span style=3D"text-decoration: none;">References</span></span></h1>
<ol>
<li><a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3Dll=
GgO74uXMI&amp;pp=3DygUgdmVua2F0IHN1YnJhbWFuaWFtIGRldm94eDkgeWVhcnM%3D" titl=
e=3D"Core Design Principles for Software Developers by Venkat Subramaniam" =
rel=3D"nofollow">Core Design Principles for Software Developers by Venkat S=
ubramaniam</a></li>
<li>Clean Code by Robert C. Martin</li>
</ol>
<h1 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-BestPractices">Best Practices</h1>
<h2 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-POJOsinsteadofHashMap">POJOs instead of Ha=
shMap</h2>
<p>Using POJOs (Plain Old Java Objects) instead of HashMap to model input o=
r output for a method is a best practice in software development.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Type Safety: </strong>POJOs provide strong typing, ensuring tha=
t the data structure adheres to a specific schema. With HashMap, you lose t=
ype safety because it uses generic key-value pairs, which can lead to runti=
me errors.</li>
<li><strong> Readability:</strong>&nbsp;POJOs make the code more readable a=
nd self-explanatory. The fields in a POJO clearly define the structure of t=
he data, whereas a HashMap requires additional documentation or inspection =
to understand its keys and values.</li>
<li><strong>Maintainability:</strong> POJOs are easier to maintain and exte=
nd. You can add fields, annotations (e.g., @JsonProperty), or validation ru=
les without affecting other parts of the code. With HashMap, changes to key=
s or values require updates across all usages, increasing the risk of error=
s.</li>
<li><strong>Serialization/Deserialization: </strong>POJOs work seamlessly w=
ith libraries like Jackson or Gson for JSON serialization/deserialization. =
Using HashMap for this purpose can lead to issues, as it doesn't enforce a =
specific structure.</li>
<li><strong>Validation:</strong> POJOs can include validation annotations (=
e.g., @NotNull, @Size) to enforce constraints on the data. HashMap does not=
 support such validations directly.</li>
<li><strong>IDE Support: </strong>POJOs benefit from IDE features like auto=
completion, refactoring, and type checking. With HashMap, you lose these ad=
vantages because keys are typically strings and not strongly typed.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Not Recommended</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs">public void proces=
sData(HashMap&lt;String, Object&gt; input) {
    String name =3D (String) input.get("name");
    int age =3D (int) input.get("age");
    // Logic here
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Recommended</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs">public class UserD=
ata {
    private String name;
    private int age;
}

public class UserResponse {
=09private response:
}

public UserResponse processData(UserData input) {
=09UserResponse userResponse =3D new UserResponse();
    String name =3D input.getName();
    int age =3D input.getAge();
    // Logic here
=09return userResponse;
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-UseAbstractClassesforCommonFunctionalities=
">Use Abstract Classes for Common Functionalities</h2>
<p>Try to extract the common functionalities into a Base/Parent class so co=
de changes/fixes can be done at one place.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended</strong></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs">// TBD: add exampl=
e</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-Validations">Validations</h2>
<h3 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-CommonValidations">Common Validations</h3>
<p><strong>Problem:</strong> Code duplication in validators leads to redund=
ancy, making the code harder to maintain and prone to errors. If a change i=
s required, it must be applied to multiple places, increasing the risk of i=
nconsistencies.<br><strong>Solution:</strong> Extract common validation log=
ic into a base validator class. This base class can provide reusable method=
s or utilities that child validators can inherit and use. This approach pro=
motes code reuse and reduces duplication.</p>
<p>Example</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs">// Base Validator
public abstract class BaseValidator {
    protected void validateNotNull(Object value, String fieldName) {
        if (value =3D=3D null) {
            throw new InvalidInputDataException(fieldName + " cannot be nul=
l");
        }
    }

    protected void validateStringNotEmpty(String value, String fieldName) {
        if (value =3D=3D null || value.trim().isEmpty()) {
            throw new InvalidInputDataException(fieldName + " cannot be emp=
ty");
        }
    }
}

// Specific Validator
public class RetriggerQARRequestValidator extends BaseValidator {
    public void validate(RetriggerQARRequest request) {
        validateNotNull(request.getIdpTraceId(), "idpTraceId");
        validateStringNotEmpty(request.getRequestType(), "requestType");
        // Additional specific validations
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-OnlyValidations">Only Validations</h3>
<p><strong>Problem:</strong> Validators performing tasks beyond validation =
(e.g., creating responses) violate the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP=
). This makes the code harder to test and maintain.<br><strong>Solution:</s=
trong> Validators should focus solely on validation. Any response creation =
or transformation logic should be handled in a separate layer, such as a se=
rvice or utility class.</p>
<p>Example</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs">// Validator
public class RetriggerQARRequestValidator extends BaseValidator {
    public void validate(RetriggerQARRequest request) {
        validateNotNull(request.getIdpTraceId(), "idpTraceId");
        validateStringNotEmpty(request.getRequestType(), "requestType");
    }
}

// Service
public class RetriggerQARService {
    public RetriggerQARResponse processRequest(RetriggerQARRequest request)=
 {
        // Business logic and response creation
        return new RetriggerQARResponse("Success");
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-ValidationExceptionHandling">Validation Ex=
ception Handling</h3>
<p><strong>Problem:</strong> Mixing validation logic with HTTP response cre=
ation tightly couples the validation layer with the HTTP layer, making the =
code less modular and harder to test.<br><strong>Solution:</strong> Use exc=
eptions to signal validation failures. These exceptions should be caught an=
d translated into appropriate HTTP responses by a centralized exception han=
dler (e.g., a @ControllerAdvice in Spring Boot).</p>
<p>Example</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs">// Custom Exceptio=
n
public class InvalidInputDataException extends RuntimeException {
    public InvalidInputDataException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

// Exception Handler
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(InvalidInputDataException.class)
    public ResponseEntity&lt;String&gt; handleInvalidInputDataException(Inv=
alidInputDataException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body(ex.getMessage());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity&lt;String&gt; handleGenericException(Exception ex=
) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR).body=
("An error occurred");
    }
}

// Validator Usage
public class RetriggerQARRequestValidator extends BaseValidator {
    public void validate(RetriggerQARRequest request) {
        validateNotNull(request.getIdpTraceId(), "idpTraceId");
        validateStringNotEmpty(request.getRequestType(), "requestType");
    }
}

// Controller
@Controller
public class RetriggerQARResourceImpl {
    @Autowired
    private RetriggerQARRequestValidator validator;

    @PostMapping("/retriggerQAR")
    public ResponseEntity&lt;RetriggerQARResponse&gt; retriggerQAR(@Request=
Body RetriggerQARRequest request) {
        validator.validate(request);
        // Proceed with processing
        return ResponseEntity.ok(new RetriggerQARResponse("Success"));
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"CleanCodeBestPractices-Avoidduplicatedcode">Avoid duplicated code=
&nbsp;</h1>
<p>1. Lombok annotations can be used to replace explicit getter and setter =
methods in resource requests, and response files, simplifying the code and =
improving readability. However, if <code>equals</code> and <code>hashCode</=
code> functions rely on specific fields for computation, Lombok annotations=
 should be avoided for them to prevent potential data inconsistencies in En=
tity classes.</p>
<p>Example : User class has setter, getter , toString , equals and hashcode=
 functions.&nbsp;</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs">public class User =
{
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public User() {}

     public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name =3D name;
    }
    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
    public void setAge(int age) {
        this.age =3D age;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "User{name=3D'" + name + "', age=3D" + age + "}";
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this =3D=3D obj) return true;
        if (obj =3D=3D null || getClass() !=3D obj.getClass()) return false=
;
        User user =3D (User) obj;
        return age =3D=3D user.age &amp;&amp; name.equals(user.name);
    }

       @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(name, age);
    }
}

</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Usuage of Lombok annotations</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs">import lombok.Data=
;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;

@Data
@NoArgsConstructor
public class User {
    private String name;
    private int age;
}
// @Data automatically generates getters, setters, toString(), equals(), an=
d hashCode().
// @NoArgsConstructor ensures the creation of a no-argument constructor.</p=
re>
</div>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<p>2. Move the validation logic for <code>hmResult</code> and <code>hmRespo=
nse</code> to dedicated utility classes, ensuring they are invoked centrall=
y for better maintainability and reuse. Also, hmDBusInput params, Catch and=
 Finaly block code can be moved to Utility classes.</p>
<p>&nbsp; Example:&nbsp;</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: true; theme: Emacs" data-theme=3D"Emacs"> =09=09if (hmResul=
t =3D=3D null || hmResult.isEmpty()) {
=09=09=09=09stAppInfo =3D "Null Response returned from associationBaseObj.v=
alidateInputData()";
=09=09=09=09hmResult =3D CommonErrorMap.makeCategoryMap(CErrorDefs.ERR_SYS_=
APPL_NUM, stAppInfo);
=09=09=09=09logger.info("{}{}AAH_06.a : {}", sClassName, sMethodName, stApp=
Info);
=09=09=09=09return hmResult;
=09=09}

=09=09=09stAppStatusCode =3D (String) hmResult.get(MPSDefs.APP_STATUS_CODE)=
;

=09if (!(CommonDefs.COMMON_SUCCESS.equals(stAppStatusCode))) {
=09=09=09=09stAppInfo =3D (String) hmResult.get(CommonDefs.COMMON_APP_INFO)=
;
=09=09=09=09logger.info("{}{}AAH_06.b : {}", sClassName, sMethodName, stApp=
Info);
=09=09=09=09hmResult =3D CommonErrorMap.makeCategoryMap(Integer.parseInt(st=
AppStatusCode), stAppInfo);
=09=09=09=09return hmResult;
=09=09}

 &nbsp; &nbsp;//The validation logic currently present in multiple helper c=
lasses should be consolidated into utility classes for better reusability a=
nd maintainability.
=09
=09hmResult =3D CommonUtilHelper.handleResponseForInvalidDataErr(hmResult, =
logger, sClassName, sMethodName);
=09=09if (!CommonDefs.COMMON_SUCCESS.equals(hmResult.get(CErrorDefs.COMMON_=
APP_STATUS_CODE))) {
=09=09=09return hmResult;
=09=09}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<p>3. If multiple classes share similar methods, use abstract classes or in=
terfaces to define shared behavior.</p>
<p>4. Move repeated logic into utility/helper classes or methods. For examp=
le, error response generation and validation logic can be centralized.</p>
<p>5. Identify existing implementations and consolidate them into utility c=
lasses for better reuse. Upon deployment, review the new functionality for =
duplicate code, refactor common logic, and move it to utility classes to en=
hance maintainability.</p>
<p><br></p>
<p><br></p>
<p><br></p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
